# No Backwards Compatibility

When updating or modifying a feature, treat the change as a **full rewrite** of the relevant portions. Do not preserve old behavior, shims, feature flags, or parallel code paths “just in case.”

## Code

- **Remove** replaced APIs, tables, routes, env vars, migrations patterns, and dead branches—do not leave them commented out or marked deprecated.
- **Do not add** compatibility layers (adapters, fallbacks, dual reads/writes, version checks) unless the user explicitly asks for them.
- **Update all call sites** in the same change; do not leave consumers on the old path.

## Documentation

- Document **only the current** behavior and constraints.
- **Do not** mention prior designs, renamed concepts, or removed flows—even to contrast or explain a change.
- **Avoid** phrases like “supersedes…”, “replaces the old…”, “no longer includes…”, “legacy…”, “formerly…”, or “migration from…”.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD – compatibility shim
function getOrgId(user: User): string {
  return user.orgId ?? user.organization_id; // old field
}

// ✅ GOOD – single source of truth
function getOrgId(user: User): string {
  return user.orgId;
}
```

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD, references legacy process directly -->
Signup now uses `org_id` in metadata. The legacy default-role trigger was removed.

<!-- ❌ BAD,still references old functionality -->
Signup requires `org_id` and `role_id` in user metadata; triggers do not assign a default role.

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
Signup requires `org_id` and `role_id` in user metadata.
```

## When changing existing work

If you find legacy code, references, or doc callouts while implementing a feature change, **delete them** as part of the same change rather than working around them.
